#
# Per-rig overrides for the slider subprocess.
#
# Sourced from config/sliderconf.tcl at the end of its startup, so the
# slider namespace and its subscriptions to ain/vals and slider/virtual
# are already in place. Use this file to pick which ain channel the
# physical slider is wired to and to set calibration defaults that
# match the pot and workspace on this rig.
#

# --- Input source ------------------------------------------------------
#
# Controls which input path drives slider/position. Prevents the virtual
# slider and real hardware from competing (whichever fires last wins).
#
#   "ain"      - only the ain/vals hardware path publishes (rig default)
#   "trackpad" - use USB trackpad as source for slider
#   "virtual"  - only the virtual path publishes (dev / simulation)
#   "auto"     - both paths active, last writer wins (default out of box)
#
# On a rig with a wired slider, set to "ain":
# slider::set_source ain
#
# On a dev laptop with no hardware, set to "virtual":
# slider::set_source virtual

# --- Channel mapping ---------------------------------------------------
#
# chan_x = 0 means the slider pot is wired to ain channel 0.
# chan_y = -1 disables the y axis (1D slider setup; the default).
# For a 2D setup (two pots, or a non-centering thumbstick), set chan_y.
#
# slider::set_chan_x 0
# slider::set_chan_y -1

# --- Calibration -------------------------------------------------------
#
# Linear model: out = scale * ((raw - center) minus deadzone), with
# optional invert and symmetric clamp.
#
#   center:    raw ADC value when the slider is at its neutral /
#              experimentally-zero position. For a 12-bit ADC with a
#              symmetric pot wired between 0 and 3.3V, center ~= 2048.
#              Alternatively, park the slider where you want zero and
#              call slider::set_current_as_center.
#
#   scale:     output units per raw ADC count. If your experiment's
#              world uses visual degrees and full pot deflection covers
#              +/- 10 deg of travel, scale = 10.0 / 2048 ~= 0.00488.
#
#   deadzone:  raw ADC counts around center to treat as zero. Useful
#              for cheap pots with noisy rest position.
#
#   invert:    flip the sign of the axis (if the pot is wired such
#              that increasing pot position decreases the output).
#
#   limit:     symmetric clamp on the calibrated output, < 0 to
#              disable. Useful to prevent runaway if the subject
#              deflects to the mechanical stop.
#
# slider::set_center_x   2048.0
# slider::set_scale_x    0.00488
# slider::set_deadzone_x 20.0
# slider::set_invert_x   0
# slider::set_limit_x    10.0

# ---- extio joystick + select button (source `extio`) ------------------------
#
# An analog stick digitized by an extio box, with a button for the commit.
# Two box-side groups, addressed by LABEL so any box on the rig can carry them:
#
#   ain group  `stick`         two channels, the X/Y axes
#   DI  group  `stick_select`  one pin, the select/commit button
#
# Define those on the box and the slider comes alive -- no binding to keep in
# step, the same convention emconf uses for the analog eye group `eye`.
#
# WHY A BUTTON RATHER THAN "RETURNS TO CENTRE". A spring-return stick sweeps
# back through decreasing radius, so committing on "radius fell below
# threshold" samples the angle where it is least determined. The button commits
# while the stick is still deflected. A stick's own push-select works, but
# pressing DOWN THROUGH the stick nudges X/Y at exactly the sampled instant --
# a separate button avoids that entirely and is worth it for real data.
#
# ---- 1. box side --------------------------------------------------------
# (extio-config page, or `dservctl -H <host> extio "extio_cfg_set <box> ..."`)
#
#   ain/group/<i>/label stick        ain/group/<i>/channels 0,1
#   ain/group/<i>/mode onchange      ain/group/<i>/deadband 4
#   ain/rate 100                     ain/oversample 64
#   pin/<n>/mode in_pullup           pin/<n>/debounce_ms 25
#   group/<j>/label stick_select     group/<j>/pins <n>
#
# WHY THOSE NUMBERS:
#   onchange is only safe because 64x hardware oversampling puts resting noise
#   under ~0.5 counts, so a deadband of 4 is ~8 sigma and a still stick emits
#   NOTHING (verified: 0 publishes in 15 s at rest). Without the oversampling
#   this is the publish storm that used to take a box off the air.
#
#   The base rate must stay at or under 200 Hz. On-change publishing is gated
#   at one block per 5 ms, so a 250 Hz base is silently HALVED mid-gesture --
#   ain/dbg/throttled counts it, but the samples are gone.
#
#   Channels 0/1 are dedicated pads on the MCXN947 (A0/A1) and need no pin
#   mode. Only channels 2-5 (pins 14-17) need `pin <n> mode ain`.
#
# ---- 2. rig side --------------------------------------------------------
#
# SET THE SOURCE EXPLICITLY on any rig with more than one slider input wired.
# The default "auto" permits every path, so a trackpad and a stick would both
# publish slider/position and the last writer would win.
#
# slider::set_source   extio
# slider::set_chan_x   0        ;# column index in the `stick` block, NOT an ADC channel
# slider::set_chan_y   1
# slider::set_scale_x  0.00488  ;# 10 / 2048 -> full deflection ~= +-10 units
# slider::set_scale_y  0.00488
# slider::set_limit_x  -1.0     ;# see the asymmetry note below
# slider::set_limit_y  -1.0
# slider::set_swipe_threshold 1.0
#
# ---- 3. MEASURE THE CENTRE. It is not 2048. -----------------------------
#
# A real stick rests wherever its pots rest -- 1968/1951 on the bench unit, not
# 2048. Left at 2048 that one sat at magnitude 0.60 against a threshold of 1.0,
# i.e. 60% of the way to ENGAGED while untouched: engagement asymmetric, every
# committed angle biased toward the resting offset, and the whole thing reading
# as a "twitchy stick" rather than a wrong constant.
#
#   slider::set_center_x <measured>
#   slider::set_center_y <measured>
#
# or, better, call slider::set_current_as_center with the stick at rest.
# CAVEAT in onchange mode: at rest the box publishes nothing, so the proc
# captures whatever the last movement left behind. Nudge the stick, let it
# settle, THEN call it.
#
# ASYMMETRY THIS CREATES: with the centre off 2048 the two directions no longer
# have equal travel (centre 1968 -> +10.38 one way, -9.60 the other). A
# symmetric `limit` therefore clips one side only, which distorts the angle at
# large deflection. Either disable the limit (-1, fine for swipe, where only
# the angle matters) or set it to the SMALLER half-range.
#
# ---- 4. axis signs are per-stick wiring, so measure them too -------------
#
# Do NOT carry the trackpad's invert_y over -- that 1 is there because trackpad
# Y grows downward, which says nothing about how your stick is wired.
#
#   slider::set_invert_x 0
#   slider::set_invert_y 0
#
# Push RIGHT and commit -> slider/swipe/angle should read ~0 rad.
# Push UP    and commit -> ~1.571 rad (90 deg).
# Flip the corresponding invert for whichever axis disagrees; if the angle is
# consistently 180 deg out, flip both.
#
# ---- 5. verifying ------------------------------------------------------
#
#   dservctl -H <host> -c "dservGet slider/swipe/engaged"   ;# 1 while deflected
#   dservctl -H <host> -c "dservGet slider/swipe/angle"     ;# radians, on commit
#
# NB dservGet returns a scalar float as a FORMATTED STRING, not raw bytes --
# `binary scan` on it yields ~0 and looks exactly like a broken commit. Array
# datapoints (state/ain/<group>) DO come back as bytes. This asymmetry has
# already cost one false bug report.
#
# The protocol still sets -mode/-release/-threshold via ::ess::slider_init, and
# -threshold OVERRIDES whatever is set here -- so the value above is only the
# floor for the no-protocol case.

# Print the resulting settings so the log clearly shows the current
# per-rig calibration.
puts "slider (local): [dservGet slider/settings]"
